/*
RMSTEST_43b - the control for 43a: identical map, no accumulate_connections
Feeds: preview-design Sec.15 item 22, CREATION_PLAN 4.8 (S5's all-pairs A*)
Run 43a as well. Neither file means anything on its own.

QUESTION
S5 is the preview's remaining hot spot. `create_connect_all_lands` over L lands
runs one A* search per PAIR, so ~25 lands is 300 searches and one corpus map
spends 2.8s of its 3.8s there. The known fix is one multi-source Dijkstra per
SOURCE land, C(L,2) searches down to L.

It is not implemented because of one line in connections.ts:

    const terrainOf = accumulating ? grid.terrain : startOfS5Terrain;

`grid.terrain` is a live reference and paths paint immediately, so under
accumulate_connections pair 2's search sees the terrain pair 1 laid down.
Batching per source land would compute every path against one frozen grid. That
is a behaviour change, Sec.6.5 specifies the accumulating behaviour, and
CLAUDE.md says specs are authoritative - so it needs agreeing, not improvising.

Sec.15 item 22 offers two ways out and the first one is WRONG. It says to batch
only when accumulate_connections is off, "and both slow corpus maps qualify".
`24hr_Caverns.rms` - the 2.8s map, the one that motivated the whole item -
turns accumulate_connections ON at line 463 and writes its
create_connect_all_lands at line 517. So the conditional fix would leave the
worst map exactly as slow as it is now. Only 2 of 57 corpus maps use the toggle
at all and the expensive one is not the one that qualifies.

THE THIRD OPTION, WHICH IS WHAT THIS RUN IS FOR
Our implementation makes the flag mean live reads INCLUDING pair to pair inside
a single command. Nobody measured that. The flag's documented job is to carry
painted terrain forward, and "forward" could just as well mean to the next
COMMAND. If the engine only accumulates between commands, then batching per
source land is correct unconditionally, the deviation disappears, and 4.8 stops
being a spec question and becomes an afternoon.

METHOD
Six small lands in a line, placed by land_position so the layout is the same in
both files. One create_connect_all_lands, which is 15 pairs. Grass is expensive
and road is cheap, by a factor of 20, so a path will happily detour a long way
onto existing road rather than cut across grass.

  A path that can see earlier paths' road reuses it. One that cannot pays full
  grass every time and lays fresh road along its own straight line.

So the whole reading is ONE NUMBER: how many tiles of ROAD exist at the end.

43a has accumulate_connections. 43b is byte-identical without that line. The
script has exactly one connection command on purpose, so the flag has no
between-command effect available to it and anything it changes is within-command
by construction.

GENERATE BOTH AT NORMAL (200), any player count, and read them the same way.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. 43a road count clearly BELOW 43b. The engine accumulates within a command.
   Item 22's batching is a real deviation; implement it only when the flag is
   off, and accept that Caverns stays slow, or find a third design.
2. 43a and 43b road counts equal within run-to-run noise. The engine does NOT
   accumulate within a command. Batch per source land unconditionally, and
   correct connections.ts's per-pair live read while you are there, since it
   would then be modelling something the engine does not do.
3. Either file comes back with almost no road at all. The cost setup did not
   bite - most likely terrain_cost is being read from the wrong terrain, or the
   lands are too close for a detour to ever pay. Fix the fixture, not the
   question.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>"
  The terrain histogram is the whole reading. Compare ROAD between the two.

RUN EACH THREE TIMES. Land growth is random even with land_position fixed, so a
single pair of numbers is not a difference. Prediction 1 expects a gap far
larger than that spread; if the gap is the same size as the spread, that is
prediction 2.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  create_land { terrain_type GRASS land_position 15 50 base_size 4 number_of_tiles 40 }
  create_land { terrain_type GRASS land_position 30 50 base_size 4 number_of_tiles 40 }
  create_land { terrain_type GRASS land_position 45 50 base_size 4 number_of_tiles 40 }
  create_land { terrain_type GRASS land_position 60 50 base_size 4 number_of_tiles 40 }
  create_land { terrain_type GRASS land_position 75 50 base_size 4 number_of_tiles 40 }
  create_land { terrain_type GRASS land_position 90 50 base_size 4 number_of_tiles 40 }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

  /* THE CONTROL. 43a is this file plus one accumulate_connections line here.
     Every path is computed against the terrain as it stood before S5, so this
     is the road count for six lands connected with no reuse. */
  create_connect_all_lands
  {
    replace_terrain GRASS ROAD
    terrain_cost GRASS 20
    terrain_cost ROAD 1
  }
